(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How are seborrheic keratoses diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Clinically, with supportive management; cryotherapy may help if itchy or inflamed, though they recur","By biopsy in every case, with excision because they are easily mistaken for neoplasms","Clinically, with excision and negative margins required for every symptomatic lesion","Clinically, with topical fluorouracil applied for two to four weeks as field therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — no treatment is required for asymptomatic lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man had anaphylaxis after a wasp sting last summer and has a positive skin test. Which is the most appropriate long-term management?","choices":["Carry an oral antihistamine and take it at the first sign of any sting reaction","Take a daily leukotriene antagonist through the summer months of each year","Avoid all outdoor activity between the months of July and September each year","Carry an epinephrine auto-injector and be referred for desensitisation therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — immunotherapy is specified for fire ant hypersensitivity as well. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How common is basal cell carcinoma, and which ultraviolet exposure pattern drives it?","choices":["It is the second most common skin cancer, driven by prolonged CUMULATIVE ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people","It is the fourth most common cancer overall, driven by intense intermittent ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people","It is a rare cancer, driven by human herpesvirus 8 in the setting of immune senescence","It is the most common form of cancer, driven by intense, INTERMITTENT ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — intermittent, unlike the cumulative pattern of squamous cell carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has folliculitis of the thighs that has not cleared after two weeks of topical clindamycin. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin oral isotretinoin, since follicular disease unresponsive to topicals needs it","Reassure and stop treatment, since folliculitis resolves without any intervention","Unroof a pustule for Gram stain and culture, since this is now a resistant case","Switch to a different topical antibiotic and review again in a further two weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — culture, potassium hydroxide mount, nasal swab and biopsy are all available. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with numerous solar lentigines asks whether the birth control she took for years contributed. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Birth control use raises the risk of melanoma within these lesions specifically","Birth control use is among the named associations, alongside older age, sun damage, ephelides and tanning","Birth control use is unrelated, since solar lentigines are caused by sun exposure and nothing else","Birth control use is the principal cause, and stopping it will make the lesions fade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — 90% of people have solar lentigines by the age of fifty. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has multiple 1 to 2 mm skin-coloured papules symmetrically distributed on both lower eyelids and upper cheeks. They are asymptomatic and appeared around puberty. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sebaceous hyperplasia","Syringoma","Xanthelasma","Milia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — benign eccrine duct neoplasms, appearing at puberty, females more than males. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are the first-line treatments for an infantile hemangioma?","choices":["Beta-blockers and corticosteroids; both are named first line","Intralesional corticosteroid alone, with fluorouracil added if the lesion continues to enlarge","Surgical excision in every case, performed before the proliferative phase has finished","Cryotherapy with liquid nitrogen, repeated until the lesion has flattened completely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — propranolol by mouth or timolol topically, whose mechanism is not well understood, and corticosteroids given topically, intralesionally or orally to slow growth and shrink the lesion. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has recurrent tapioca-like vesicles on his palms confirmed clinically as dyshidrotic eczema. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["A topical antifungal","Oral prednisone tapered over three weeks","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","A low-potency topical corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High potency is specified first line here, with systemic corticosteroids reserved for severe cases. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What does the deck say about school exclusion in tinea capitis?","choices":["Generally unnecessary once effective therapy has begun","The child must be excluded until fungal culture is negative","The child must be excluded for the full duration of oral therapy","Exclusion is required only if a kerion is present"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — follow local policy. And complete the oral course even when itching or scaling improves early. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the natural course of hot tub folliculitis?","choices":["Resolving only after the affected follicles are drained","Usually self-limiting, clearing in two to ten days","Progressive without oral ciprofloxacin in every patient","Chronic and relapsing for months after a single exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ciprofloxacin is reserved for widespread or resistant cases. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient has an unusual, poorly defined, extensive eruption on the trunk. She has been applying a topical corticosteroid for months; it settles while she uses it and flares when she stops, prompting more steroid. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea incognito","Nummular eczema","Psoriasis","An id reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — tinea whose appearance is altered by inappropriate steroid treatment. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Nail clipping with periodic acid-Schiff stain evaluates which suspected condition?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Onychomycosis","Psoriatic nail disease","Subungual melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which tests may be used in acute paronychia and what does each exclude?","choices":["Nasal swab for staphylococcal carriage, plucked hair for dermatophytes, biopsy for follicular disease","Gram stain and culture for the bacterial cause, potassium hydroxide for candida, Tzanck smear for herpetic whitlow","Wood's lamp for coral-red fluorescence, potassium hydroxide for dermatophytes, biopsy for malignancy","Blood cultures for bacteraemia, creatine phosphokinase for muscle damage, imaging for osteomyelitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is usually a clinical diagnosis, so testing is directed rather than routine. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What self-examination does the deck ask melanoma patients to perform, and which sites must be included?","choices":["Monthly self-examination of the trunk and limbs only, since acral, nail and scalp melanoma are too rare to warrant routine checking","No self-examination at all, since surveillance of the skin, scalp, nails and acral sites is entirely the specialist team's responsibility","Monthly self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles and nails","Annual self-examination using the recognition mnemonic alone, limited to the sun-exposed skin of the face, arms and upper chest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the acral and scalp sites are easy to miss. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has several round scaly plaques on his forearms. The lesions are uniform throughout without central clearing, and he has been applying an over-the-counter antifungal for three weeks without improvement. Which is the best initial diagnostic test?","choices":["Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence","Serum immunoglobulin E level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis is the main differential, and it must be excluded before committing to a diagnosis of nummular eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"When is surgical treatment appropriate in vitiligo, and what else is named in management?","choices":["Tissue or cellular grafting only for highly stable disease, alongside psychological intervention and cosmetic therapies","Grafting at first presentation in every patient, since earlier surgery gives a better repigmentation result","Grafting whenever phototherapy has failed after five weeks of narrow band ultraviolet B","Grafting is never used, since the depigmented areas repigment spontaneously over time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — combining topical therapy with phototherapy is described as ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the only reliable way to diagnose a keratoacanthoma?","choices":["Biopsy","Dermoscopy","Clinical appearance alone","Response to a trial of cryotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the clinical differential includes three malignancies. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Mental health challenges or cognitive impairment appear in the lecture under which heading?","choices":["Indications for the naturalistic approach","Elements of the FRAMES strategy","Steps of the clinical reasoning process","Barriers to treatment and medication adherence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are listed among the barriers, alongside cost, low health literacy, cultural or religious beliefs, fear of side effects, transportation and poor follow-up. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has a firm pink dome-shaped papule on the forearm. His parents ask whether it could be on his palm or in his mouth too. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Spitz naevi occur only on the lower extremity, mainly the thigh","Spitz naevi spare the palms, soles and mucous membranes","Spitz naevi occur most commonly on the palms and the soles","Spitz naevi occur only on the dorsal hands, scalp and sacrum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they occur on face, neck, trunk and extremities. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which element of FRAMES places the decision to change with the patient?","choices":["Feedback about personal risk","Empathetic style","Promote self-efficacy","Responsibility of the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FRAMES includes responsibility of the patient as a distinct element, alongside feedback, advice, a menu of options, an empathetic style and promoting self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"For which specific behaviours does the lecture point to counselling guidelines in Bates' Chapter 7?","choices":["Diet, exercise, and sleep hygiene","Medication timing, appointment keeping, and the sharing of records between providers","Weight control, hydration, and screen time","Unhealthy alcohol use, tobacco smoking cessation, and sexually transmitted infections"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture directs students to specific counselling guidelines for adults on these three. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which preventive measures are advised in hidradenitis suppurativa?","choices":["Avoid heat, cleanse daily with antibacterial soap or benzoyl peroxide wash, lose weight, stop smoking","Avoid all water contact, apply a barrier ointment daily, gain weight and use an occlusive dressing","Shower immediately after any hot tub use and monitor the disinfectant levels in the water","Avoid sharing towels or razors and isolate from school until treatment has been underway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with avoiding constrictive clothing and considering laser hair removal. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What does the deck warn against for benign warts likely to resolve on their own?","choices":["Salicylic acid at any concentration","Excessive freezing or destructive therapy","Any period of observation without treatment","Trimming the surface of the lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — do not over-treat a self-limiting lesion. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"Which layers does cellulitis involve, and which organisms cause it?","choices":["The deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue, from Group A streptococci or Staphylococcus aureus","The upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, almost always from Group A streptococci","The superficial epidermis alone, from Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes","The stratum corneum of intertriginous skin, from Corynebacterium minutissimum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It may be purulent or non-purulent, which changes the antibiotic choice. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"What should the history cover in a patient with spider angiomas?","choices":["Pregnancies, hormone use, alcohol history, and medications that carry a risk of liver damage","Birth history, feeding and weight gain, and any family history of vascular birthmarks","Footwear, occupation and use of tools or sports equipment","Immobility, incontinence and nutritional status"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion can be a marker of estrogen excess or liver disease. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has thickened discoloured toenails and you decide to confirm the diagnosis before prescribing. Which tests does the lecture list, and how should the sample be taken?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide microscopy or fungal culture only; sample the most distal free edge of the nail plate before any trimming of the onycholytic nail is performed","Serology for dermatophyte antibodies or a blood culture; no local sampling of the nail is required at any stage","A Wood lamp examination followed by biopsy of the nail matrix; sample only after six weeks of topical therapy has failed","Potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid–Schiff stain of clippings, culture, or amplification testing; sample the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sample subungual debris after trimming the onycholytic nail. Sampling proximally is the part most often missed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who smokes has had painful nodules and draining sinuses in both axillae and the groin for two years, flaring five or six times a year, with meshlike scars between them. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Recurrent furunculosis of the axilla","Cutaneous Crohn's disease of the groin","Erythrasma of the axilla and the groin","Hidradenitis suppurativa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — typical lesions, apocrine distribution and recurrence more than twice in six months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"How should a suspected basal cell carcinoma be sampled?","choices":["With a sentinel lymph node biopsy at the time of diagnosis","With a shave or punch biopsy","With a wide local excision carrying 1 cm margins","With a potassium hydroxide preparation of the surface scale"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the histologic subtype then dictates treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"In VINDICATE, the letter E stands for which category?","choices":["Eosinophilic","Endocrine (metabolic)","Environmental exposure","Embolic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. E closes the mnemonic as Endocrine, with metabolic given in parentheses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which long-term measures are offered after a sting-induced anaphylactic reaction?","choices":["Carry oral doxycycline, to be started at the first sign of any systemic symptoms","Carry an epinephrine auto-injector, and desensitisation therapy where the skin test is positive","Carry oral antihistamines, with a short course of prednisolone kept for emergencies","Carry a topical corticosteroid, with local anaesthetic injection for pain control"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — immunotherapy is specified for fire ant hypersensitivity. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with perioral dermatitis has stopped her topical corticosteroid. Which is the most appropriate counselling point about what happens next?","choices":["The eruption should clear completely within 48 hours of stopping","She should resume the steroid if the rash worsens at all","The condition will not recur once the corticosteroid has been stopped once","The eruption may temporarily worsen after corticosteroid withdrawal before it improves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — telling her in advance is what stops her restarting the steroid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which populations and regions are particularly affected by cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["The Great Lakes region, and paddy workers and rice farmers of the Far East","The American Midwest and Southeast, particularly people using attics and storage areas","The Pacific Northwest, particularly people working in basements and around wood piles","The southeastern and south central states, particularly rural dwellers in spring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — exposure to cercaria-infested water is what all of them share. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A patient asks how likely his actinically induced squamous cell carcinoma is to spread. Which figure does the deck give, and what raises it?","choices":["An estimated 30 to 70%, raised further by high-risk sites or features, immunosuppression, multiple tumours, recurrence, or nodal involvement","An estimated 3 to 7%, and nothing raises it further, since metastatic risk is fixed at the moment of diagnosis whatever the site or immune status","Under 0.1%, raised only by nodal involvement already present at the time of diagnosis","An estimated 3 to 7%, raised substantially by high-risk sites or features, immunosuppression, multiple tumours, recurrence, or nodal involvement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the baseline is modest but modifiable. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with newly confirmed dermatitis herpetiformis is about to start dapsone. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Perform HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 typing to confirm susceptibility","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status before the first dose","Check thyroid-stimulating hormone and anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Arrange small bowel biopsy before any treatment is commenced"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, then monitor the blood count for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What do multiple Spitz naevi raise the possibility of?","choices":["Dysplastic naevus syndrome","An underlying gastrointestinal malignancy","A familial cancer syndrome","Neurocutaneous melanosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a single lesion carries no such implication. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus has a painful corn on his fifth toe. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Excise the lesion with a five-millimetre margin","Refer him to podiatry","Start an over-the-counter salicylic acid product and review in a month","Pare the lesion down in clinic and advise wider footwear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck flags the diabetic patient specifically for podiatry referral. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What mechanism underlies the most common form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["A delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction to a contacted allergen","Autoantibody deposition along the epithelial basement membrane zone","Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation","A paraneoplastic process driven by an underlying gastric adenocarcinoma secreting growth factors that act on the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the insulin-resistant or benign form is the commonest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy brushed against a caterpillar in the garden and now has multiple itchy erythematous papules arranged in linear streaks on his forearm. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A processionary caterpillar reaction with urticaria and angio-oedema","Cutaneous larva migrans acquired from soil in the same garden","Erucism, or caterpillar dermatitis, from a gypsy moth caterpillar","An asp or puss caterpillar sting, the most poisonous of the group"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a pruritic dermatitis with papules in linear streaks. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"In what circumstance do hypertrophic scars characteristically occur?","choices":["When a wound is left to heal by secondary intention rather than closed","When the patient has dark skin, which is strongly associated with them","When a wound becomes infected during the proliferative phase of healing","When scars cross joints or skin creases at a right angle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and they develop soon after surgery rather than months later. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How are severe or extensive allergic contact dermatitis eruptions treated?","choices":["High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks, because early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound","High-potency topical steroids alone regardless of how extensive the eruption is across the body surface area","A five-day course of oral corticosteroids without taper","Oral antihistamines alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Limited eruptions can be managed with high-potency topical steroids. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has confirmed bedbug bites and asks what will get rid of them. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Leaving the room unoccupied for a month will starve the insects out of the property","Vacuuming floors, carpets and upholstery thoroughly is sufficient without any chemical treatment","A professional exterminator is necessary; treatment of the bites themselves is symptomatic","Washing all bedding at 60 degrees Celsius and bagging clothing for fourteen days will clear it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical antiseptic or antibiotic for secondary infection, steroids or antihistamines for itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A patient with acute zoster has mild pain. Which comfort measures does the lecture describe?","choices":["Acetaminophen or a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug, with cool compresses, calamine, loose clothing, and lesion coverage","A long-acting opioid from the outset, with warm compresses, occlusive dressings, tight clothing, and the lesions left uncovered","A systemic corticosteroid from the outset, with cool compresses, calamine, and lesion coverage","No analgesia at all, with cool compresses, calamine, loose clothing, and lesion coverage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lesion coverage also reduces exposure of contacts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"What causes pityriasis versicolor, and is it contagious?","choices":["Infection with Trichophyton rubrum from shared towels; it is contagious","An autoimmune destruction of melanocytes; it is not contagious","Overgrowth of Malassezia, which normally lives on skin; NOT contagious","Infection with Candida albicans acquired from close contact; it is contagious"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lipid-dependent Malassezia species. More common with heat, humidity, oily skin, sweating, immunosuppression or corticosteroid exposure. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"In whom is body louse infestation typically found, and how does it persist?","choices":["Schoolchildren sharing hats and hair accessories, persisting through repeated close head contact","Sexually active adults across all social levels, often with a concurrent sexually transmitted infection","Long-term care residents who are elderly and immunosuppressed, persisting through staff carriage","Homeless people, refugees, war victims and those in crowded conditions, persisting via unwashed clothing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — transmission is through contaminated clothing and bedding. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with a body mass index in the 97th percentile has velvety, darkened, thickened skin at the back of the neck and in both axillae. Which is the most appropriate initial testing?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation of scrapings from the affected skin","Skin biopsy of the posterior neck for histological confirmation","Fasting glucose, haemoglobin A1c, fasting insulin and a lipid panel","Computed tomography of chest, abdomen and pelvis with upper endoscopy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the finding is a visible marker of insulin resistance and warrants metabolic assessment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is a macule defined in this lecture?","choices":["A flat elevated plateau lesion measuring greater than one centimetre across","A flat lesion less than one centimetre, without elevation or depression","A flat lesion less than five millimetres","An elevated solid lesion less than one centimetre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patch is the same lesion larger than one centimetre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Describe the clinical appearance of ephelides and the main differential.","choices":["Beige to black papules and plaques 2 to 20 mm across, with melanoma as the main differential","White non-scaly macules with distinct margins, with tinea versicolor as the main differential","Deep blue macules under 1 cm across, with nodular melanoma as the main differential","Small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across, with lentigines as the main differential"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they are asymptomatic and the diagnosis is clinical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which lesion-directed therapy does the deck give for actinic keratosis, and what happens to the lesion afterwards?","choices":["Topical fluorouracil — the lesion crusts and disappears over 10 to 14 days; preferred for isolated lesions with clear borders","Surgical excision with 5 mm margins — the wound heals over 10 to 14 days; preferred for any lesion regardless of number","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy — the lesion crusts and disappears over 10 to 14 days; preferred for isolated or few lesions with clear borders","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy — the lesion blisters and resolves within 24 hours; preferred for widespread disease across a whole zone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lesion-directed, for small numbers. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotics are used for furuncles and carbuncles when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected?","choices":["Penicillin V, erythromycin or clarithromycin","Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin or moxifloxacin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin or doxycycline","Dicloxacillin, cephalexin or amoxicillin-clavulanate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy for the sensitive organism is dicloxacillin or cephalexin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which examination finding separates tinea barbae from bacterial folliculitis?","choices":["The hair is easily removed in tinea barbae","The lesions are painful in tinea barbae and painless in folliculitis","Tinea barbae spares the upper lip","Tinea barbae produces fever and lymphadenopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and bacterial culture can be performed to rule folliculitis out. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How are Stevens-Johnson syndrome, the overlap state and toxic epidermal necrolysis separated?","choices":["By whether the Nikolsky sign is positive or negative at presentation","By body surface area of epidermal detachment: under 10%, 10 to 30%, and over 30%","By the presence of mucosal involvement, which occurs only in the severe form","By whether the trigger was a drug or an infection in each case"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same disease spectrum divided by extent of detachment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with facial papules typical of dermatosis papulosa nigrans has one lesion that looks different from the rest. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure him, since every lesion in this condition is benign without exception","Arrange dermoscopy of the whole face and repeat it again in twelve months","Biopsy that lesion, since biopsy is indicated where the diagnosis is uncertain","Treat all the lesions with cryotherapy and review the atypical one afterwards"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is otherwise clinical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does polymorphous light eruption typically present?","choices":["An exaggerated sunburn on first exposure to a new medication","Persistent eczematous change in chronically exposed skin of older males","Papules appearing thirty minutes to hours after exposure on the décolletage, forearms and dorsal hands, sparing chronically exposed skin","Painful erythema developing within minutes of ultraviolet exposure and resolving within an hour of moving into the shade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, resolving in seven to ten days without scarring and recurring each spring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"How is Rocky Mountain spotted fever treated, including in pregnancy and young children?","choices":["Amoxicillin for children and pregnant patients, with doxycycline reserved for adults only","Azithromycin for all patients, since doxycycline is contraindicated below eight years of age","Intravenous ceftriaxone for all patients, with oral therapy reserved for prophylaxis","Doxycycline for adults, pregnant patients and children, with desensitisation where it is contraindicated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 100 mg twice daily for adults and 2.2 mg per kilogram twice daily for children, for 5 to 10 days. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"How broad is the referral requirement in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Essentially every specialty, given the multisystem burden","Dermatology alone, since the disease is confined to the skin","Genetics alone, since the diagnosis is made on a genetic panel","No routine referral, since the condition is managed in primary care"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — palliative care, genetics and family support are all involved. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What opportunity does the deck say a new lesion in a sun-exposed area creates?","choices":["To counsel on sunscreen, peak-hour sun avoidance and skin checks","To screen for hyperlipidemia and assess cardiac risk","To refer to podiatry if the patient is diabetic","To advise avoiding cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the general education point the deck attaches to every benign lesion in a sun-exposed area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"You suspect a basal cell carcinoma on a patient's shoulder. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic step, and why does it matter which subtype comes back?","choices":["Wide local excision with 1 cm margins, because tissue diagnosis and treatment are achieved together","Sentinel lymph node biopsy, because nodal status determines treatment selection","Shave or punch biopsy, because the HISTOLOGIC subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection","Shave or punch biopsy, because the CLINICAL subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — histology, not clinical appearance, drives the plan. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A patient treated for squamous cell carcinoma of the scalp returns with new numbness and tingling in the distribution of the scar. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Reassure, because sensory change is expected permanently after any scalp excision","Repeat topical fluorouracil to the scar and review in three months","Arrange routine review in twelve months as part of annual surveillance","Refer urgently, because neurologic symptoms are one of the deck's urgent referral triggers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they raise concern for perineural invasion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How do urushiol lesions present, and over what time course?","choices":["Well-demarcated plaques shaped like the object contacted, erupting within minutes of exposure and clearing within a day","Diffuse urticarial wheals resolving within 24 hours","Greasy scaling patches in sebum-rich areas over several weeks","Intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions, erupting within 4 to 96 hours and persisting up to 3 weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the linear pattern comes from grazing the plant in passing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What is the hardening phenomenon in polymorphous light eruption?","choices":["Progressive scarring of affected sites over successive seasons","Loss of pigment in the affected areas after each episode","Improvement through the summer as tolerance develops after repeated exposure","Thickening and hardening of the skin from repeated scratching of the eruption"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is also why prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B in spring is the most effective preventive strategy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 104"}]};
